# DESCRIPTION

The plumber routes messages between programs, as Plan 9's does.
A program that has something another should open, such as a file name or a URL, writes a message to `send`; the plumber chooses a port by its rules, and the program that reads the port gets the message.
A manifest mounts its tree at `/mnt/plumb`.

A message is the `plumb(6)` format, six lines and then data:

```text
src
dst
wdir
type
attr
ndata
<ndata bytes of data>
```

`src` is the sender, `dst` the port wanted (the plumber sets it to the port chosen), `wdir` the sender's directory, `type` such as `text`, `attr` pairs `name=value` apart by blanks (a value holding a blank, a quote or an equals sign is quoted with `'`, a quote inside doubled), and `ndata` the number of bytes of data that follow, which are the last line's.
A message is one write; it is refused if the bytes after the sixth line are not `ndata`.

## Rules

A ruleset is lines up to a blank one: patterns, then actions.
The first ruleset in force whose patterns all hold takes the message, and its actions apply.
`#` starts a comment.
A line `NAME=value` outside a ruleset defines `$NAME` for the rulesets after it.
Words are split at blanks, and `'` quotes one (`''` for a quote inside).

A pattern is an object, a verb and one argument.
The objects are `src`, `dst`, `wdir`, `type`, `data`, `attr` and `arg`.

: `is`
  The field equals the argument.
  A `type` argument ending in `*` is a prefix: `type is image/*`.
  `attr is name=value` holds if the message has that attribute.
: `matches`
  The argument is a regular expression, as Plan 9's `regexp(6)` has them (literals, `.`, classes `[a-z]`, `[^a-z]`, `*`, `+`, `?`, `|`, groups, `^`, `$`), that the whole field must match.
  Its groups are `$1` to `$9`, and the whole match `$0`.
: `isfile`, `isdir`
  The argument, taken against `wdir` if it is not an absolute path, names a file or a directory.
  It is then `$file` or `$dir`.
: `set`
  The field becomes the argument.
  `attr set` replaces all the attributes.
: `add`, `delete`
  `attr add name=value` adds or replaces an attribute, and `attr delete name` removes one.

The arguments of a pattern and of the actions may use `$0` to `$9`, `$src`, `$dst`, `$wdir`, `$type`, `$data`, `$file`, `$dir` and the `NAME`s defined.

The actions:

: `plumb to PORT`
  The message goes to the port.
  A ruleset needs one.
: `plumb client APP ARG ...`
  If no one reads the port, start APP with the arguments and hold the message until the port is opened.
: `plumb start APP ARG ...`
  If no one reads the port, start APP and drop the message.

The rules in force when the plumber starts send a compiler's `file:line` and a text file's name to `edit` (the first adds an `addr` attribute), a page of the manual, `name(N)`, to `man`, which guide(4) reads, a URL to `web`, a `mailto:` address to `mail`, and an `image/` type to `image`.

## Ports

A port is a file for each `plumb to` in the rules, made when the rules are read and never taken away.
Each channel open on a port gets its own copy of every message sent to it after the open.
A read returns one message, or as much of it as the read asks for, the rest on the next read, and waits if there is none.

When a message is sent to a port nobody has open, `plumb client` starts the program and holds the message for the first channel to open the port; at most 32 are held, the oldest dropped.
`plumb start` starts the program and drops the message.
With neither, the write to `send` fails.
A program is started from `/bin`, never through a shell, and is given the plumber's tree at `/mnt/plumb` and the plumber's `/bin`.
The plumber logs each message it routes or refuses, and each program it starts.

# EXAMPLES

A ruleset to write to `rules`: text beginning `hello` goes to a port `greet`, and if no one reads it `cat` is started to read it.

```text
type is text
data matches 'hello .*'
plumb to greet
plumb client cat /mnt/plumb/greet
```

# FILES

: `/mnt/plumb/send`
  Write a message here to have it routed.
  The write is refused with `invalid` if the bytes are not a message, `notFound` if no ruleset takes it, and `io` if the port has no reader and the ruleset starts nothing, or the program could not be started.
  Reads give nothing.
: `/mnt/plumb/rules`
  Reads give the rules in force.
  A write adds its rules as a ruleset after them; the whole set is parsed again, and if there is an error in it the write is refused as `invalid` and the rules stay as they were.
: `/mnt/plumb/edit`
  A port of the rules in force at the start: files and `file:line` places.
: `/mnt/plumb/web`
  A port of those rules: URLs.
: `/mnt/plumb/mail`
  A port of those rules: `mailto:` addresses.
: `/mnt/plumb/image`
  A port of those rules: images.
  A ruleset added to `rules` makes a file for each port it names that is new.

